BODY = '''<p>The base of a cane decides how much it steadies you and how much it slows you down. A standard cane has one tip. A quad cane has four short legs on a flat base. Two sources lay out the trade: a January 2023 edition of "Elder Care" from the University of Arizona Center on Aging, written for clinicians, and a March 2020 AARP page by Barbara Stepko. This page puts them side by side.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>A cane is a medical aid.</strong> The Arizona authors close by urging clinicians to teach the type, the fit and the use of a cane, because a patient who buys one alone gets none of that. A doctor or physical therapist can watch you walk and say which base suits you.</p>

<h2>The Three Cane Types</h2>
<p>The Arizona edition names three main types, and says canes are used by one in ten older adults.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standard (single-point) cane.</strong> The most widely used type. Its main purpose is to improve balance by widening your base of support. It is not appropriate for someone who needs help bearing weight, and the authors say a standard cane can support up to 25% of a person's weight.</li>
<li><strong>Offset cane.</strong> Shaped so that your weight sits over the axis of the cane, which allows occasional weight bearing. The authors say it is often recommended for people with arthritis in the hip or knee.</li>
<li><strong>Multiple-legged cane.</strong> Three or four short legs on a base, called a quad or a tri cane. The Arizona edition says these provide more support than standard or offset canes and can bear more of a person's weight. Our <a href="/best-canes/">cane guide</a> compares an offset cane, a quad-base cane and folding canes.</li>
</ul>
<p>The same page says anyone who needs major weight-bearing support generally needs a walker or hemi-walker instead of a cane. For that choice, see <a href="/articles/cane-walker-or-rollator/">cane, walker or rollator</a>.</p>

<h2>What a Quad Cane Gives You</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>More support.</strong> AARP describes the quad cane as giving a wider base of support for balance, against a single-point cane for people who need just a little steadying. The Arizona authors say quad canes can be used to unload weight, for example with an uneven gait from osteoarthritis, or to improve balance after a neurological condition such as a stroke.</li>
<li><strong>It stands up on its own.</strong> Both sources say so. The Arizona edition adds that this frees your hands and lets you pick the cane up without bending down.</li>
<li><strong>A bent handle on some models.</strong> The Arizona edition describes a "sit-to-stand" quad cane with a handle you can grip at two levels, so you can push on the lower part when you rise from a chair.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What You Give Up</h2>
<p>Both sources name the same cost. For a quad cane to work as designed, all the legs need to touch the ground together, and that makes the gait slower. The Arizona authors say this is the main difficulty, particularly when the cane takes weight. AARP says a quad cane is heavier and clunkier than a single-point cane and usually calls for a slower walking pattern, because all four tips must touch the ground with each step.</p>

<h2>Narrow Base or Wide Base</h2>
<p>The Arizona edition says quad canes come with a narrow base or a wide base, and the wide base gives more support. It then describes a way out of the slow gait: in some cases, people change from a wide-base quad cane to a narrow-base quad cane or even a tri cane. The faster a person walks, the fewer legs are needed and the closer together the legs can be, though the trade-off is some loss of stability while the cane takes weight. Neither source gives base sizes in inches, so check the listing for the cane you are considering, and ask a therapist which base suits your stride.</p>

<h2>Fitting Any Cane</h2>
<p>The Arizona edition says one rule applies to any type of cane. The right length is the distance from the floor to the crease of your wrist when your arm hangs loosely at your side. With the tip on the floor about 6 inches out from the edge of your toes, your elbow should bend about 20 degrees. AARP gives a similar range, a top that reaches the wrist with the elbow bent between 10 and 30 degrees, and warns that a cane that is too tall or too low can disrupt the way you walk.</p>
<p>Wooden canes have a fixed length and need careful fitting before you use them. Aluminum canes usually adjust. Our <a href="/tools/cane-and-walker-height-checker/">cane and walker height checker</a> compares your wrist height with the ranges of the canes in our guide.</p>
<h3>Which hand</h3>
<p>The Arizona tips say that for weight bearing, the cane goes on the side opposite the injured leg, while for balance people usually use their dominant side. In the text it adds that the cane is generally held by the arm on the same side as the stronger leg. AARP says to use it in the hand opposite the painful joint. A quad cane also has to be set for a right or a left hand, which the Arizona edition says you do by turning the axis 180 degrees.</p>

<h2>Everyday Use</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check the tips.</strong> The Arizona edition says canes should have non-skid rubber tips, checked often and replaced when worn. It tells clinicians to check the tips and the height-adjustment screws too, because nobody should put weight on an unsteady cane.</li>
<li><strong>Steps.</strong> The Arizona edition says the unaffected leg takes the first full weight-bearing step on level ground and going up a step, and the affected leg goes down a step first, in line with the cane.</li>
<li><strong>Handles.</strong> The same page says umbrella-style handles have been linked to carpal tunnel syndrome, and that a padded horizontal palm grip is less likely to cause it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cane Questions</h2>
<h3>Is a quad cane better than a standard cane?</h3>
<p>It gives more support and bears more weight, and it stands on its own. A standard cane is lighter and faster to use. The Arizona edition recommends a standard cane when you need help with balance but not weight bearing, and a multiple-legged cane when you need more substantial weight-bearing support.</p>
<h3>How do I know which base I need?</h3>
<p>Neither source gives a rule you can apply at home. The Arizona edition links the choice to how much weight you put through the cane and how fast you walk. A physical therapist can watch you walk and recommend a narrow or wide base.</p>
<h3>When should I replace the rubber tips?</h3>
<p>The Arizona edition says to check them often and replace them when they wear out.</p>
<h3>Can a cane stop me from falling?</h3>
<p>The Arizona edition says using a mobility device does not necessarily mean fewer falls. It adds that among people who fall at home, most do not have an assistive device with them, and that people who do not use a device sustain more severe injuries when they fall. See the <a href="/articles/do-home-safety-changes-prevent-falls/">evidence on home safety changes</a>.</p>

<h2>Choosing a Cane</h2>
<p>Our <a href="/best-canes/">guide to walking canes</a> compares five canes on height range, weight limit and base, including a self-standing quad-base cane.</p>'''

